<p>The field was named for Hatton, North Dakota, native {% person "carl-ben-eielson" %}Carl Ben Eielson{% endperson %}. In April 1928, Eielson and {% person "hubert-wilkins" %}Hubert Wilkins{% endperson %} flew a Lockheed Vega from Point Barrow, Alaska, to Spitsbergen, Norway, becoming the first people to cross the North Pole by air. Expected to last about 20 hours, the flight took six days after the aviators encountered a severe winter storm.</p>

<p>Eielson enlisted in the U.S. Air Corps in 1917 and later served in the aviation section of the U.S. Signal Corps during World War I. After the war, he briefly returned to North Dakota. He was recruited as an Alaska airmail pilot in 1921 and went on to make Arctic aviation history. Eielson and his mechanic died in December 1929 while attempting to rescue 15 people aboard the cargo ship <em>Nanuk</em>, which was trapped in ice off Siberia's North Cape.</p>

<p>Although Mandan's present airport no longer bears Eielson's name, the U.S. Air Force operates Eielson Air Force Base 25 miles southeast of Fairbanks, Alaska. Established in 1939 as a cold-weather aircraft-testing station, the base became home to the 354th Fighter Wing. A World War II Liberty ship and numerous schools in Alaska and North Dakota were also named for Carl Benjamin Eielson.</p>
